::JOUR_U04_L07_Q_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.MED.02] Source: Media Package: Lunch Schedule Debate. Media Text A: News Brief. The school is considering a ten-minute extension to lunch. The proposal would shorten afternoon advisory by ten minutes. The principal says the school is reviewing hallway crowding, lunch line data, and afternoon instructional time before making a decision. Media Text B: Editorial Excerpt Supporting the Change. Students need a longer lunch because current lines leave some students with fewer than ten minutes to eat. A survey from the student newspaper found that 72 percent of respondents support a longer lunch. Supporters argue that a longer lunch would reduce rushing and help students return to class more focused. Media Text C: Editorial Excerpt Opposing the Change. The school should not lengthen lunch if it reduces advisory. Advisory gives students time for grade checks, teacher conferences, and makeup work. Opponents argue that the better solution is to improve line flow instead of removing academic support time. Media Features: The package includes a comparison chart, a hallway photo caption, and a sidebar titled Questions Still Under Review. Question 1: Which choice best identifies the shared issue? {
~A. The shared issue is whether students should stop eating lunch at school.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
=B. The shared issue is whether lunch should be extended by ten minutes even though advisory would be shortened.#Correct. Step 1: identify the media literacy skill in the standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to connect structure, central idea, purpose, claim, evidence, rhetoric, mood, or audience effect to the best answer. This answer is strongest because it explains the source rather than relying on personal preference.
~C. The shared issue is whether advisory should replace all classes.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~D. The shared issue is whether the principal has already made a final decision.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
}
::JOUR_U04_L07_Q_Q02::[MLA.JOUR.MED.04] Source: Media Package: Lunch Schedule Debate. Media Text A: News Brief. The school is considering a ten-minute extension to lunch. The proposal would shorten afternoon advisory by ten minutes. The principal says the school is reviewing hallway crowding, lunch line data, and afternoon instructional time before making a decision. Media Text B: Editorial Excerpt Supporting the Change. Students need a longer lunch because current lines leave some students with fewer than ten minutes to eat. A survey from the student newspaper found that 72 percent of respondents support a longer lunch. Supporters argue that a longer lunch would reduce rushing and help students return to class more focused. Media Text C: Editorial Excerpt Opposing the Change. The school should not lengthen lunch if it reduces advisory. Advisory gives students time for grade checks, teacher conferences, and makeup work. Opponents argue that the better solution is to improve line flow instead of removing academic support time. Media Features: The package includes a comparison chart, a hallway photo caption, and a sidebar titled Questions Still Under Review. Question 2: Which evidence supports the lunch extension claim? {
~A. The support claim is proven because every student wants longer lunch.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~B. The lunch extension is supported only by the writer opinion.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~C. The support claim is based on hallway crowding only.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
=D. The survey showing 72 percent support and the detail about fewer than ten minutes to eat support the lunch extension.#Correct. Step 1: identify the media literacy skill in the standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to connect structure, central idea, purpose, claim, evidence, rhetoric, mood, or audience effect to the best answer. This answer is strongest because it explains the source rather than relying on personal preference.
}
